<p>The living room has more surfaces on show than any other room: walls, a TV wall, a ceiling, a large floor, upholstery and curtains. Each one faces different wear, from sticky fingers on the walls to afternoon sun on the sofa. This guide matches each surface with the materials that suit it. It is part of the <a href="/rooms/living-room/">living room design guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Materials at a glance</h2>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Best everyday choice</th><th>Feature option</th><th>Avoid</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Walls</td><td>Washable emulsion</td><td>Texture paint, limewash, wallpaper, panelling</td><td>Flat distemper in a family room</td></tr>
    <tr><td>TV wall</td><td>Laminate on BWR plywood</td><td>Veneer, fluted panels, stone-look slab</td><td>MDF on a damp outside wall</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>Gypsum board, peripheral</td><td>Wooden rafters, cove and tray ceilings</td><td>POP in damp areas</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Floor</td><td>Vitrified tile</td><td>Indian marble, large-format slabs, engineered wood</td><td>Polished tiles in homes with elders</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sofa</td><td>Performance polyester blend</td><td>Leather, bouclé, velvet</td><td>Untreated linen with small children</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Curtains</td><td>Cotton or poly-cotton with lining</td><td>Sheer + blackout layers</td><td>Unlined fabrics on west windows</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Walls</h2>
<p>Use a washable premium emulsion on most walls; it can be wiped without leaving a patch. Choose one feature wall for texture: <a href="/wall-design/texture-paint/">texture paint</a>, <a href="/trends/limewash-textures/">limewash</a>, <a href="/wall-design/wallpaper-guide/">wallpaper</a> or <a href="/wall-design/wall-panelling/">panelling</a>. On outside walls, check for damp before any panel or wallpaper goes on. Colour pairings are in <a href="/colour/living-room-colour-combination/">living room colour combinations</a>.</p>

<h2>The TV wall</h2>
<p>The TV wall carries a unit, wiring and often a light, so it needs a strong backing. Laminate or veneer on 18 mm BWR plywood is the standard. Fluted MDF panels give texture in dry rooms; WPC or PVC panels suit walls that might get damp. Stone-look laminates and thin porcelain slabs give a marble wall without the weight. See <a href="/wall-design/pvc-vs-wpc-wall-panels/">PVC vs WPC panels</a> and <a href="/cost/tv-unit-cost/">TV unit cost</a>.</p>
