<h2>Ceiling</h2>
<p>A gypsum board ceiling on a metal frame is the default: smooth, quick and inexpensive at about ₹85–160 per sq ft. Use a peripheral design to hide AC piping and keep the centre at full height. See the <a href="/false-ceiling/">false ceiling guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Floor</h2>
<p>Vitrified tile is the practical choice; Indian marble and large-format slabs are the premium ones. Engineered wood works only in dry, air-conditioned rooms. Full comparisons are in <a href="/rooms/living-room-flooring/">living room flooring</a>.</p>

<h2>Upholstery and curtains</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sofas:</strong> performance blends or leatherette for families; bouclé and velvet for low-traffic rooms. See the <a href="/furniture/sofa-buying-guide/">sofa buying guide</a> and <a href="/trends/boucle-fabrics/">bouclé fabrics</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Curtains:</strong> a sheer layer for daytime privacy and a lined or blackout layer for heat, especially on west-facing windows.</li>
  <li><strong>Rugs:</strong> flat-weave cotton or wool blends are easy to wash; shaggy rugs trap dust.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Materials for humid months</h2>
<p>From June to October, humidity in South Indian cities stays high. Seal every cut edge of plywood panels, avoid solid wood panels on outside walls, keep upholstery off damp walls and choose fabrics that dry quickly. Homes in South-East Bengaluru also face fine dust from roads and construction, which is easier to live with on matte, closed surfaces. Our execution partner builds <?= huma('bommasandra', 'living room interiors in Bommasandra') ?>, Electronic City and Chandapura with these choices as standard.</p>
